00:00:01.735 --> 00:00:03.303
A verdict in the copyright

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:05.705
trial involving pop singer Ed Sheeran.

00:00:05.739 --> 00:00:06.239
He's accused

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.974
of stealing the musical composition

00:00:07.974 --> 00:00:09.175
of his Grammy winning song,

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:10.410
thinking out Loud,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.978
from Marvin Gaye's classic

00:00:11.978 --> 00:00:13.246
Let's Get It On.

00:00:13.246 --> 00:00:13.880
CNN's Kelly

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.582
Wallace is outside the courthouse.

00:00:15.582 --> 00:00:18.451
Chloe, what is the verdict?

00:00:19.686 --> 00:00:22.288
Yes, well, as you said, the verdict is in

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:24.391
and the jury has found Ed Sheeran

00:00:24.624 --> 00:00:26.259
not liable.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.395
He did not infringe on Marvin

00:00:28.395 --> 00:00:31.364
Gaye's Let's Get It on his 1973 hit.

00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:33.600
So the verdict has just come out

00:00:33.633 --> 00:00:34.367
moments ago.

00:00:34.367 --> 00:00:36.136
We are waiting for Ed Sheeran,

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:38.071
his attorneys, and Katherine Townsend

00:00:38.071 --> 00:00:40.040
and her attorneys to make their way out.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:40.907
We're hoping that

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:43.143
perhaps they will address reporters

00:00:43.143 --> 00:00:44.477
right here behind me.

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:46.112
But this is a very significant moment

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:47.714
for the music industry.

00:00:47.714 --> 00:00:49.382
Ed Sheeran said

00:00:49.382 --> 00:00:50.350
when he took the stand

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.418
in his testimony

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:53.019
that this was a common four

00:00:53.019 --> 00:00:54.254
chord progression.

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:56.389
And attorneys for Ed Sheeran

00:00:56.990 --> 00:00:57.857
laid it all out

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:00.060
over the course of a few days

00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:02.862
showing that this four chord progression

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:04.531
that is in Let's Get It On.

00:01:04.531 --> 00:01:06.366
That's also in Ed Sheeran's

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:07.133
Thinking Out Loud

00:01:07.133 --> 00:01:08.234
was commonplace

00:01:08.234 --> 00:01:10.136
that it was in dozens of songs

00:01:10.136 --> 00:01:11.971
before Thinking Out Loud

00:01:11.971 --> 00:01:14.107
and also in songs that predate

00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:15.542
Let's Get It On.

00:01:15.542 --> 00:01:16.376
And so Ed

00:01:16.376 --> 00:01:17.377
Sheeran had said,

00:01:17.377 --> 00:01:19.412
if you all do this

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:19.979
and make it

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:22.082
so that every time a musician

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.683
has to go to the Townsend family

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.852
to use this for chord progression

00:01:25.952 --> 00:01:27.153
that I'm done.

00:01:27.153 --> 00:01:29.289
I can't even be a musician anymore.

00:01:29.456 --> 00:01:30.857
And it made

00:01:30.857 --> 00:01:32.192
they made a very strong

00:01:32.192 --> 00:01:33.126
closing arguments.

00:01:33.126 --> 00:01:34.294
Ed Sheeran's legal team

00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:36.529
saying that this would stifle

00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:39.265
the creativity of musicians everywhere

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.301
and that that is not what copyright

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.602
law is all about.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:45.271
It is to obviously protect music

00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:46.806
and protect

00:01:46.806 --> 00:01:48.374
one's intellectual property,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:49.442
but at the same time

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.577
allow for creativity.

00:01:50.577 --> 00:01:52.112
So it's a big moment.

00:01:52.112 --> 00:01:53.113
We are expecting, I'm sure,

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:55.014
to hopefully say a few words.

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:56.049
And obviously this news

